<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Wu of Jin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">236–290</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Sima Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Wu of Jin, born Sima Yan, was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a>. In 266 he forced the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> emperor to abdicate, and in 280 he conquered Eastern Wu, briefly reuniting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/three-kingdoms-period">Three Kingdoms period</a>. His enfeoffment of princes with armies prepared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-wu-of-jin.jpg" alt="Emperor Wu of Jin" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yan Li-pen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Wu_Di.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sima Yan was born in 236, the eldest son of Sima Zhao and Wang Yuanji. Sima Zhao was then a mid-level official of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> and the son of the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-yi">Sima Yi</a>. After Sima Shi died in March 255, Sima Zhao became regent and the paramount authority in the Wei government. His father hesitated between him and his brother Sima You as heir, but several high officials favoured Sima Yan. When Sima Zhao died on 6 September 265, Sima Yan became King of Jin the next day, and on 4 February 266 he forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-huan">Cao Huan</a> to abdicate. On 8 February 266 he declared himself emperor of the Jin dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Believing that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a> had been doomed by its weak princes, Emperor Wu made princes of many uncles, cousins, brothers and sons in February 266, each with independent military command. He also softened the penal law, but nobles received simple rebukes while commoners saw no real reduction. In 267 he made Sima Zhong crown prince, who appeared developmentally disabled, and in 272 chose Jia Nanfeng, daughter of Jia Chong, as his princess.</p><p>A revolt by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> chief Tufa Shujineng from 270 held his attention until Ma Long put it down in 279. He then sent a six-pronged attack on Eastern Wu, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-hao">Sun Hao</a> surrendered on 1 May 280. After the conquest he ordered provincial governors to become purely civilian and regional militias disbanded. In 281 he took 5,000 women from Sun Hao's palace into his own and gave them more attention than state affairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As Emperor Wu grew ill in 289 he considered Yang Jun and his uncle Sima Liang as regents. After he wrote his will, Yang Jun seized it and promulgated another naming himself alone as regent. Emperor Wu died on 16 May 290. Historians commonly view him as benevolent but wasteful, too tolerant of the corruption of the aristocratic clans. His princes, given armies, fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-eight-princes">War of the Eight Princes</a> soon after his death, which led to the uprisings of the Five Barbarians that destroyed the Western Jin.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Wu of Jin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It tells of the death of Guan Yu, Liu Bei's defeat at Yiling, Zhuge Liang's campaigns against Wei, and the later fall of Shu and Wu to the Jin dynasty founded by <b>Sima Yan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/romance-of-the-three-kingdoms">Romance of the Three Kingdoms · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>The failed coup strengthened the Sima clan, and Sima Zhao's son <b>Sima Yan</b> usurped the Wei throne in 266.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-2">2 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Wei conquered Shu Han in 263, and in 266 <b>Sima Yan</b> forced Cao Huan to abdicate, founding the Western Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/cao-wei">Cao Wei</a></li><li><blockquote>After decades in which the Sima family dominated Cao Wei, <b>Sima Yan</b> forced the last Wei emperor to abdicate in 266 and founded the Jin dynasty, ruling from Luoyang as Emperor Wu.</blockquote><a href="/state/western-jin">Western Jin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=271" aria-label="Luoyang on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7405 909 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Emperor Wu of Jin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7855 1179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7855 1179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.66,112.42&amp;year=271">What was here in 271 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
